{"type":"message","id":"f96eaa58","parentId":"6a5f2fc9","timestamp":"2026-05-26T13:43:56.488Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"exec_ts_AY89a1_PHuoaWOipWzwolxbM8la8AywjOldaL_W6DTTSu-48SZa6E3plIWGPinS3YFvpNQ4ThgeWwZyr4tjLRgfTROSK1d1lHr9iH81q_RvwTUZtC7WZf0hguUg6r-d47_emqa5Gjiv-7Nrr3rgatBWcxmCvISHFZyV1xGbGyiRYc75AqR9xCkoSQCTUWZVu1vb9T8M3p4YIamHQ0nT8r22a0fVLXXRRNzU1F6m08Ggr1ai9NMD1azRruzZoiN0DczYVsB6ruTsq2gsrDORWohgZfdrHp-VP80G98RyQoOyM07mHKTlvDwhenURHbqYxv1Z42pvOEwXxvGr-8OFXyPvsUT1wwyEnCunD_mhsIKiwgOA6nKA5YmTQEEi5p2LMysb1mLz6PKlaQvuLusmo99tnqE9_yRk1XBjiE9HG-646d2SK1oaCfhfOhNqxjde3hVeT3JPhalE-X94GVTNRMFP8lzkTT8-ePWmYUyVgKqAU3KGpTlcDn2BZudF-Ifu9gNA4ns8LF38HyTaRy51gHyG76Iji0hklUnC0tHFw7joLEdZnPfiCJcyIQIvUf517nZZyE17jpYqvA_0S5bQ7WcrcctAQhGU2LKNiiGVbK3DmZnZ2ynOLEYeYIAdjIXtgL8vPoz5Q2aWnbMo36Eg8ZaKfHyQBn9JA9Trsaz84bx2D2m13lJ3dfndAI9GMH-ePDDr0gQAAL6W47WBAtc-9wjgH15GOhlQ9TNSricmxrIiDoxaljChlLjoczP4kjuus4omKoc6QDQFAyLTAk_XxZWUEDwWHkKzS1cy-FUFN895CVavikUTUrfZaX7VVoUmWRPoFIQMFNSidNWTTPkQ1F9W4ogLIyAveg22GajEmkPB9aasMjOZUNShJ1Ygggjy2hkzib5DIylNDLHz4IcUc-xd8czXZvRRCUgx10jnR7DN2KoZwBtDPfFQ16W8ie9EeUSAGfBoabUPonyVm_4lEw98P2g73y98_293oFJlmDKuFxywdRnJ7eP2aVBANvml5y9aG31B1PshZYgcYE4rfvztuH7_-2a6yCiKzg_-iWcD1JyTKEvJ5QyRDl5Xff7mmFPI_k6D7ocb4kcCo_Ivizqdj3LagzRDSv2TvUk7x2OyNU1GSAqqxlLJjvSLuLj08cxIUjPCvCn1vwiJhLuxZYJqy0ZByJK6jpc3tS8ynVYHiRt6pkfokXFng84g2SW80ExQ2_NRYNJ1pPlx39gwLQJMgoV6EM576WODoirUIEH3UZngnnbDSjJbKLicaTlQkUUIviuAIGeImoeE-aZRGBgza85eltSgKEUG9uoOs5LD9CQOWvVInqMpJbg_gC_qEz9I0qtZwwME28wT2dbgv1W5oqVCSpv0GJz79kQWSP16Q51JJEszoA4sAvmy_-bgCsBh765ixw6HYdDE7UkWZ9lw1rpkmSBIFd3Ymuo8eszgndfHlT4q83mMhJqjz0Hr-GFnXp6ChRPSH583mZJFFr9IVMHPz8-Fi2WcYxKvijSyNY7tzCKoWhpYTw_6uU7Fwb_A_tf9pq5_l-WCGB8UD3DXbhZ9IJ7KOLmhC9-LtRPsMl3eKYvnafw02uD8ffpJDi1VDvL5Ga4hdwAahubxl55hN0CCPl1D8euIob8CBiXlU9qyJWvAISJjpOzrwo8RSxAn4o6ZV","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":37848,\"date\":\"2026-05-26T09:43:54\",\"date_gmt\":\"2026-05-26T13:43:54\",\"guid\":{\"rendered\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/?p=37848\",\"raw\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/?p=37848\"},\"modified\":\"2026-05-26T09:43:54\",\"modified_gmt\":\"2026-05-26T13:43:54\",\"password\":\"\",\"slug\":\"stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-tokens\",\"status\":\"publish\",\"type\":\"post\",\"link\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-tokens\\/\",\"title\":{\"raw\":\"StablR Stablecoin Exploit Freezes USDR, EURR After $13.5M Breach\",\"rendered\":\"StablR Stablecoin Exploit Freezes USDR, EURR After $13.5M Breach\"},\"content\":{\"raw\":\"<!-- wp:group {\\\"layout\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"constrained\\\",\\\"contentSize\\\":\\\"720px\\\"}} -->\\n<div class=\\\"wp-block-group\\\">\\n<!-- wp:html -->\\n<hr \\/>\\n<!-- \\/wp:html -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The <strong>StablR stablecoin exploit<\\/strong> has forced the\\nEuropean issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its\\nUSDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According\\nto a <a\\nhref=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.coindesk.com\\/markets\\/2026\\/05\\/26\\/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens\\\">statement\\nfrom the company<\\/a>, the move came after an attacker leveraged a\\ncritical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked\\ntokens.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident\\nplaces StablR\\u2019s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the\\nEuropean Union\\u2019s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into\\nfull effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens\\nare no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA\\u2019s core\\nrequirements, and is working with <a\\nhref=\\\"https:\\/\\/beincrypto.com\\/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit\\/\\\">external\\ncybersecurity firms<\\/a> to investigate the breach.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">How a Weak Multisig\\nLed to a $13.5M Exploit<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The root cause of the attack was a fundamental weakness in StablR\\u2019s\\nsmart contract architecture on Ethereum. Blockchain security firm GoPlus\\nSecurity identified a poorly configured multisignature wallet as the\\nsingle point of failure. This wallet, responsible for minting new\\ntokens, used a 1-of-3 signature threshold. This meant any transaction\\ncould be approved by just one of the three authorized signers, offering\\nminimal protection.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The attacker compromised one of these keys, granting them unilateral\\ncontrol. They added their own address as an administrator, removed the\\nlegitimate signers, and seized the minting mechanism. With this control,\\nthe attacker minted 8.35 million USDR and 4.5 million EURR. These\\nillicitly created tokens, worth $13.5 million on paper, immediately\\ndiluted the value of the legitimately backed supply. The attacker then\\nattempted to sell the tokens on decentralized exchanges, but thin\\nliquidity meant they only netted around $2.8 million after causing a\\nmassive price crash.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Market Impact and\\nRegulatory Fallout<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of\\nStablR\\u2019s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant\\nregulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident\\nshowcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the\\nenforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":3} -->\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">USDR and EURR De-Peg From\\nFiat<\\/h3>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>Following the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR\\nlost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20\\nmillion, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially\\nrecovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged\\nEURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to\\n$0.548 against the euro\\u2019s value of $1.16.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>This de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their\\ntokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them.\\nThe price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and\\nlocked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":3} -->\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Trading Halts and Exchange\\nReactions<\\/h3>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>In response to the crisis, StablR took immediate action to contain\\nthe fallout. The company confirmed it had requested that all\\ncryptocurrency exchanges halt trading, deposits, and withdrawals for\\nboth USDR and EURR. This move aims to prevent the attacker from cashing\\nout further illicitly minted tokens and to stabilize the market by\\nfreezing activity. The freeze locks in existing holders, however,\\nleaving them unable to exit their positions until the situation is\\nresolved.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":3} -->\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">MiCA Compliance Under\\nScrutiny<\\/h3>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>StablR\\u2019s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear\\nbreach of MiCA\\u2019s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid\\nreserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the\\nbedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets\\nat par value at any time.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority\\n(MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU\\u2019s Digital\\nOperational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first\\nmajor tests of MiCA\\u2019s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will\\nwatch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent\\nis set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">StablR\\u2019s Response and\\nIndustry Context<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>StablR Chief Executive Officer Gijs op de Weegh stated the company is\\nacting \\u201cwith full transparency\\u201d as the investigation continues. The firm\\nis working with law enforcement and cybersecurity experts to trace the\\nattacker. However, the path to restoring the peg or compensating holders\\nremains uncertain.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The StablR exploit is a high-profile example in a month that has been\\nrife with crypto hacks. Data platform DefiLlama has tracked over 20\\nexploits in May 2026 alone. This trend highlights the persistent\\nsecurity challenges in the decentralized finance space and the critical\\nimportance of robust, multi-layered security configurations, especially\\nfor projects that custody user funds.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Conclusion<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The <strong>StablR stablecoin exploit<\\/strong> provides a stark\\nlesson in operational security, showing how a single point of failure in\\na multisig wallet led to a catastrophic $13.5 million breach. The attack\\nnot only de-pegged USDR and EURR but also puts the issuer in direct\\nviolation of the EU\\u2019s new MiCA regulations. For traders, this incident\\nunderscores the necessity of vetting the security architecture behind\\nstablecoins, as not all are built to withstand such threats. The\\nregulatory response from Maltese authorities will now set a crucial\\nprecedent for how stablecoin failures are managed under the MiCA\\nframework.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">FAQs<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>1. What was the StablR stablecoin exploit?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The StablR stablecoin exploit was a cyberattack where an attacker\\nused a vulnerability to mint $13.5 million in unbacked USDR and EURR\\ntokens. This action caused the stablecoins to become\\nunder-collateralized and lose their 1:1 peg to their respective fiat\\ncurrencies.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>2. How did the attacker compromise StablR\\u2019s\\nsecurity?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The attacker exploited a weak 1-of-3 multisignature wallet. By\\ncompromising a single private key, they gained control, locked out the\\noriginal team, and were able to mint unlimited new tokens.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>3. What is MiCA and how does it relate to this\\nincident?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>MiCA (Markets in Crypto-Assets) is an EU regulation requiring\\nstablecoin issuers like StablR to maintain 1:1 reserves for their\\ntokens. StablR has acknowledged it is in breach of this key rule due to\\nthe attack, which will trigger a regulatory response.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>4. Are the USDR and EURR tokens still backed\\n1:1?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>No.\\u00a0StablR has confirmed that its USDR and EURR tokens are not\\ncurrently backed at a 1:1 ratio. The company has suspended redemptions\\nwhile it investigates the breach and its potential remedies.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n<\\/div>\\n<!-- \\/wp:group -->\",\"rendered\":\"\\n<div class=\\\"wp-block-group\\\"><div class=\\\"wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-13d41742 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\\\">\\n\\n<hr \\/>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The <strong>StablR stablecoin exploit<\\/strong> has forced the\\nEuropean issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its\\nUSDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According\\nto a <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.coindesk.com\\/markets\\/2026\\/05\\/26\\/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">statement\\nfrom the company<\\/a>, the move came after an attacker leveraged a\\ncritical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked\\ntokens.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident\\nplaces StablR\\u2019s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the\\nEuropean Union\\u2019s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into\\nfull effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens\\nare no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA\\u2019s core\\nrequirements, and is working with <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/beincrypto.com\\/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit\\/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">external\\ncybersecurity firms<\\/a> to investigate the breach.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">How a Weak Multisig\\nLed to a $13.5M Exploit<\\/h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The root cause of the attack was a fundamental weakness in StablR\\u2019s\\nsmart contract architecture on Ethereum. Blockchain security firm GoPlus\\nSecurity identified a poorly configured multisignature wallet as the\\nsingle point of failure. This wallet, responsible for minting new\\ntokens, used a 1-of-3 signature threshold. This meant any transaction\\ncould be approved by just one of the three authorized signers, offering\\nminimal protection.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The attacker compromised one of these keys, granting them unilat\n\n⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]\n\n:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"2048x2048\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"et-pb-post-main-image\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-400x250.jpg\",400,250,true],\"et-pb-post-main-image-fullwidth\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-1080x675.jpg\",1080,675,true],\"et-pb-portfolio-image\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-400x284.jpg\",400,284,true],\"et-pb-portfolio-module-image\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-510x382.jpg\",510,382,true],\"et-pb-portfolio-image-single\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-1080x589.jpg\",1080,589,true],\"et-pb-gallery-module-image-portrait\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-400x516.jpg\",400,516,true],\"et-pb-post-main-image-fullwidth-large\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"et-pb-image--responsive--desktop\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-1280x698.jpg\",1280,698,true],\"et-pb-image--responsive--tablet\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-980x535.jpg\",980,535,true],\"et-pb-image--responsive--phone\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-480x262.jpg\",480,262,true]},\"rttpg_author\":{\"display_name\":\"Ahmed Falah (ahmedfalah)\",\"author_link\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/author\\/ahmed\\/\"},\"rttpg_comment\":0,\"rttpg_category\":\"<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/category\\/latest-news\\/\\\" rel=\\\"category tag\\\">Latest News<\\/a>\",\"rttpg_excerpt\":\"The StablR stablecoin exploit has frozen USDR and EURR trading after an attacker minted $13.5M in unbacked tokens, violating MiCA's 1:1 backing rule.\",\"_links\":{\"self\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\",\"targetHints\":{\"allow\":[\"GET\",\"POST\",\"PUT\",\"PATCH\",\"DELETE\"]}}],\"collection\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\"}],\"about\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/types\\/post\"}],\"author\":[{\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/users\\/16\"}],\"replies\":[{\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/comments?post=37848\"}],\"version-history\":[{\"count\":1,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\\/revisions\"}],\"predecessor-version\":[{\"id\":37849,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\\/revisions\\/37849\"}],\"wp:featuredmedia\":[{\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/media\\/37847\"}],\"wp:attachment\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/media?parent=37848\"}],\"wp:term\":[{\"taxonomy\":\"category\",\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/categories?post=37848\"},{\"taxonomy\":\"post_tag\",\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/tags?post=37848\"}],\"wp:action-publish\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-unfiltered-html\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-sticky\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-assign-author\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-create-categories\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-assign-categories\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-create-tags\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-assign-tags\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"curies\":[{\"name\":\"wp\",\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/api.w.org\\/{rel}\",\"templated\":true}]}}[... 7667 more characters truncated]"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":3683,"aggregated":"{\"id\":37848,\"date\":\"2026-05-26T09:43:54\",\"date_gmt\":\"2026-05-26T13:43:54\",\"guid\":{\"rendered\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/?p=37848\",\"raw\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/?p=37848\"},\"modified\":\"2026-05-26T09:43:54\",\"modified_gmt\":\"2026-05-26T13:43:54\",\"password\":\"\",\"slug\":\"stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-tokens\",\"status\":\"publish\",\"type\":\"post\",\"link\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-tokens\\/\",\"title\":{\"raw\":\"StablR Stablecoin Exploit Freezes USDR, EURR After $13.5M Breach\",\"rendered\":\"StablR Stablecoin Exploit Freezes USDR, EURR After $13.5M Breach\"},\"content\":{\"raw\":\"<!-- wp:group {\\\"layout\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"constrained\\\",\\\"contentSize\\\":\\\"720px\\\"}} -->\\n<div class=\\\"wp-block-group\\\">\\n<!-- wp:html -->\\n<hr \\/>\\n<!-- \\/wp:html -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The <strong>StablR stablecoin exploit<\\/strong> has forced the\\nEuropean issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its\\nUSDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According\\nto a <a\\nhref=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.coindesk.com\\/markets\\/2026\\/05\\/26\\/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens\\\">statement\\nfrom the company<\\/a>, the move came after an attacker leveraged a\\ncritical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked\\ntokens.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident\\nplaces StablR\\u2019s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the\\nEuropean Union\\u2019s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into\\nfull effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens\\nare no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA\\u2019s core\\nrequirements, and is working with <a\\nhref=\\\"https:\\/\\/beincrypto.com\\/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit\\/\\\">external\\ncybersecurity firms<\\/a> to investigate the breach.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">How a Weak Multisig\\nLed to a $13.5M Exploit<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The root cause of the attack was a fundamental weakness in StablR\\u2019s\\nsmart contract architecture on Ethereum. Blockchain security firm GoPlus\\nSecurity identified a poorly configured multisignature wallet as the\\nsingle point of failure. This wallet, responsible for minting new\\ntokens, used a 1-of-3 signature threshold. This meant any transaction\\ncould be approved by just one of the three authorized signers, offering\\nminimal protection.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The attacker compromised one of these keys, granting them unilateral\\ncontrol. They added their own address as an administrator, removed the\\nlegitimate signers, and seized the minting mechanism. With this control,\\nthe attacker minted 8.35 million USDR and 4.5 million EURR. These\\nillicitly created tokens, worth $13.5 million on paper, immediately\\ndiluted the value of the legitimately backed supply. The attacker then\\nattempted to sell the tokens on decentralized exchanges, but thin\\nliquidity meant they only netted around $2.8 million after causing a\\nmassive price crash.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Market Impact and\\nRegulatory Fallout<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of\\nStablR\\u2019s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant\\nregulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident\\nshowcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the\\nenforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":3} -->\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">USDR and EURR De-Peg From\\nFiat<\\/h3>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>Following the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR\\nlost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20\\nmillion, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially\\nrecovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged\\nEURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to\\n$0.548 against the euro\\u2019s value of $1.16.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>This de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their\\ntokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them.\\nThe price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and\\nlocked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":3} -->\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Trading Halts and Exchange\\nReactions<\\/h3>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>In response to the crisis, StablR took immediate action to contain\\nthe fallout. The company confirmed it had requested that all\\ncryptocurrency exchanges halt trading, deposits, and withdrawals for\\nboth USDR and EURR. This move aims to prevent the attacker from cashing\\nout further illicitly minted tokens and to stabilize the market by\\nfreezing activity. The freeze locks in existing holders, however,\\nleaving them unable to exit their positions until the situation is\\nresolved.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":3} -->\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">MiCA Compliance Under\\nScrutiny<\\/h3>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>StablR\\u2019s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear\\nbreach of MiCA\\u2019s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid\\nreserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the\\nbedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets\\nat par value at any time.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority\\n(MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU\\u2019s Digital\\nOperational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first\\nmajor tests of MiCA\\u2019s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will\\nwatch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent\\nis set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">StablR\\u2019s Response and\\nIndustry Context<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>StablR Chief Executive Officer Gijs op de Weegh stated the company is\\nacting \\u201cwith full transparency\\u201d as the investigation continues. The firm\\nis working with law enforcement and cybersecurity experts to trace the\\nattacker. However, the path to restoring the peg or compensating holders\\nremains uncertain.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The StablR exploit is a high-profile example in a month that has been\\nrife with crypto hacks. Data platform DefiLlama has tracked over 20\\nexploits in May 2026 alone. This trend highlights the persistent\\nsecurity challenges in the decentralized finance space and the critical\\nimportance of robust, multi-layered security configurations, especially\\nfor projects that custody user funds.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Conclusion<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The <strong>StablR stablecoin exploit<\\/strong> provides a stark\\nlesson in operational security, showing how a single point of failure in\\na multisig wallet led to a catastrophic $13.5 million breach. The attack\\nnot only de-pegged USDR and EURR but also puts the issuer in direct\\nviolation of the EU\\u2019s new MiCA regulations. For traders, this incident\\nunderscores the necessity of vetting the security architecture behind\\nstablecoins, as not all are built to withstand such threats. The\\nregulatory response from Maltese authorities will now set a crucial\\nprecedent for how stablecoin failures are managed under the MiCA\\nframework.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:heading {\\\"level\\\":2} -->\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">FAQs<\\/h2>\\n<!-- \\/wp:heading -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>1. What was the StablR stablecoin exploit?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The StablR stablecoin exploit was a cyberattack where an attacker\\nused a vulnerability to mint $13.5 million in unbacked USDR and EURR\\ntokens. This action caused the stablecoins to become\\nunder-collateralized and lose their 1:1 peg to their respective fiat\\ncurrencies.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>2. How did the attacker compromise StablR\\u2019s\\nsecurity?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>The attacker exploited a weak 1-of-3 multisignature wallet. By\\ncompromising a single private key, they gained control, locked out the\\noriginal team, and were able to mint unlimited new tokens.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>3. What is MiCA and how does it relate to this\\nincident?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>MiCA (Markets in Crypto-Assets) is an EU regulation requiring\\nstablecoin issuers like StablR to maintain 1:1 reserves for their\\ntokens. StablR has acknowledged it is in breach of this key rule due to\\nthe attack, which will trigger a regulatory response.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p><strong>4. Are the USDR and EURR tokens still backed\\n1:1?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n\\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\\n<p>No.\\u00a0StablR has confirmed that its USDR and EURR tokens are not\\ncurrently backed at a 1:1 ratio. The company has suspended redemptions\\nwhile it investigates the breach and its potential remedies.<\\/p>\\n<!-- \\/wp:paragraph -->\\n<\\/div>\\n<!-- \\/wp:group -->\",\"rendered\":\"\\n<div class=\\\"wp-block-group\\\"><div class=\\\"wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-13d41742 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\\\">\\n\\n<hr \\/>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The <strong>StablR stablecoin exploit<\\/strong> has forced the\\nEuropean issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its\\nUSDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According\\nto a <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/www.coindesk.com\\/markets\\/2026\\/05\\/26\\/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">statement\\nfrom the company<\\/a>, the move came after an attacker leveraged a\\ncritical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked\\ntokens.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident\\nplaces StablR\\u2019s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the\\nEuropean Union\\u2019s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into\\nfull effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens\\nare no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA\\u2019s core\\nrequirements, and is working with <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/beincrypto.com\\/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit\\/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">external\\ncybersecurity firms<\\/a> to investigate the breach.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">How a Weak Multisig\\nLed to a $13.5M Exploit<\\/h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The root cause of the attack was a fundamental weakness in StablR\\u2019s\\nsmart contract architecture on Ethereum. Blockchain security firm GoPlus\\nSecurity identified a poorly configured multisignature wallet as the\\nsingle point of failure. This wallet, responsible for minting new\\ntokens, used a 1-of-3 signature threshold. This meant any transaction\\ncould be approved by just one of the three authorized signers, offering\\nminimal protection.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The attacker compromised one of these keys, granting them unilateral\\ncontrol. They added their own address as an administrator, removed the\\nlegitimate signers, and seized the minting mechanism. With this control,\\nthe attacker minted 8.35 million USDR and 4.5 million EURR. These\\nillicitly created tokens, worth $13.5 million on paper, immediately\\ndiluted the value of the legitimately backed supply. The attacker then\\nattempted to sell the tokens on decentralized exchanges, but thin\\nliquidity meant they only netted around $2.8 million after causing a\\nmassive price crash.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Market Impact and\\nRegulatory Fallout<\\/h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of\\nStablR\\u2019s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant\\nregulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident\\nshowcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the\\nenforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">USDR and EURR De-Peg From\\nFiat<\\/h3>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>Following the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR\\nlost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20\\nmillion, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially\\nrecovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged\\nEURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to\\n$0.548 against the euro\\u2019s value of $1.16.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>This de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their\\ntokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them.\\nThe price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and\\nlocked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Trading Halts and Exchange\\nReactions<\\/h3>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>In response to the crisis, StablR took immediate action to contain\\nthe fallout. The company confirmed it had requested that all\\ncryptocurrency exchanges halt trading, deposits, and withdrawals for\\nboth USDR and EURR. This move aims to prevent the attacker from cashing\\nout further illicitly minted tokens and to stabilize the market by\\nfreezing activity. The freeze locks in existing holders, however,\\nleaving them unable to exit their positions until the situation is\\nresolved.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h3 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">MiCA Compliance Under\\nScrutiny<\\/h3>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>StablR\\u2019s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear\\nbreach of MiCA\\u2019s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid\\nreserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the\\nbedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets\\nat par value at any time.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority\\n(MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU\\u2019s Digital\\nOperational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first\\nmajor tests of MiCA\\u2019s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will\\nwatch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent\\nis set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">StablR\\u2019s Response and\\nIndustry Context<\\/h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>StablR Chief Executive Officer Gijs op de Weegh stated the company is\\nacting \\u201cwith full transparency\\u201d as the investigation continues. The firm\\nis working with law enforcement and cybersecurity experts to trace the\\nattacker. However, the path to restoring the peg or compensating holders\\nremains uncertain.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The StablR exploit is a high-profile example in a month that has been\\nrife with crypto hacks. Data platform DefiLlama has tracked over 20\\nexploits in May 2026 alone. This trend highlights the persistent\\nsecurity challenges in the decentralized finance space and the critical\\nimportance of robust, multi-layered security configurations, especially\\nfor projects that custody user funds.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">Conclusion<\\/h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The <strong>StablR stablecoin exploit<\\/strong> provides a stark\\nlesson in operational security, showing how a single point of failure in\\na multisig wallet led to a catastrophic $13.5 million breach. The attack\\nnot only de-pegged USDR and EURR but also puts the issuer in direct\\nviolation of the EU\\u2019s new MiCA regulations. For traders, this incident\\nunderscores the necessity of vetting the security architecture behind\\nstablecoins, as not all are built to withstand such threats. The\\nregulatory response from Maltese authorities will now set a crucial\\nprecedent for how stablecoin failures are managed under the MiCA\\nframework.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\\"wp-block-heading\\\">FAQs<\\/h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><strong>1. What was the StablR stablecoin exploit?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The StablR stablecoin exploit was a cyberattack where an attacker\\nused a vulnerability to mint $13.5 million in unbacked USDR and EURR\\ntokens. This action caused the stablecoins to become\\nunder-collateralized and lose their 1:1 peg to their respective fiat\\ncurrencies.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><strong>2. How did the attacker compromise StablR\\u2019s\\nsecurity?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The attacker exploited a weak 1-of-3 multisignature wallet. By\\ncompromising a single private key, they gained control, locked out the\\noriginal team, and were able to mint unlimited new tokens.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><strong>3. What is MiCA and how does it relate to this\\nincident?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>MiCA (Markets in Crypto-Assets) is an EU regulation requiring\\nstablecoin issuers like StablR to maintain 1:1 reserves for their\\ntokens. StablR has acknowledged it is in breach of this key rule due to\\nthe attack, which will trigger a regulatory response.<\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><strong>4. Are the USDR and EURR tokens still backed\\n1:1?<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>No.\\u00a0StablR has confirmed that its USDR and EURR tokens are not\\ncurrently backed at a 1:1 ratio. The company has suspended redemptions\\nwhile it investigates the breach and its potential remedies.<\\/p>\\n\\n<\\/div><\\/div>\\n\",\"protected\":false,\"block_version\":1},\"excerpt\":{\"raw\":\"The StablR stablecoin exploit has frozen USDR and EURR trading after an attacker minted $13.5M in unbacked tokens, violating MiCA's 1:1 backing rule.\",\"rendered\":\"<p>The StablR stablecoin exploit has frozen USDR and EURR trading after an attacker minted $13.5M in unbacked tokens, violating MiCA&#8217;s 1:1 backing rule.<\\/p>\\n\",\"protected\":false},\"author\":16,\"featured_media\":37847,\"comment_status\":\"open\",\"ping_status\":\"open\",\"sticky\":false,\"template\":\"\",\"format\":\"standard\",\"meta\":{\"_et_pb_use_builder\":\"\",\"_et_pb_old_content\":\"\",\"_et_gb_content_width\":\"\",\"footnotes\":\"\"},\"categories\":[17],\"tags\":[],\"permalink_template\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/%postname%\\/\",\"generated_slug\":\"stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-usdr-eurr-after-13-5m-breach\",\"class_list\":[\"post-37848\",\"post\",\"type-post\",\"status-publish\",\"format-standard\",\"has-post-thumbnail\",\"hentry\",\"category-latest-news\"],\"rttpg_featured_image_url\":{\"full\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"landscape\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"portraits\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"thumbnail\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-150x150.jpg\",150,150,true],\"medium\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-300x164.jpg\",300,164,true],\"large\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-1024x559.jpg\",1024,559,true],\"1536x1536\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"2048x2048\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"et-pb-post-main-image\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-400x250.jpg\",400,250,true],\"et-pb-post-main-image-fullwidth\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-1080x675.jpg\",1080,675,true],\"et-pb-portfolio-image\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-400x284.jpg\",400,284,true],\"et-pb-portfolio-module-image\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-510x382.jpg\",510,382,true],\"et-pb-portfolio-image-single\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-1080x589.jpg\",1080,589,true],\"et-pb-gallery-module-image-portrait\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-400x516.jpg\",400,516,true],\"et-pb-post-main-image-fullwidth-large\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1.jpg\",1408,768,false],\"et-pb-image--responsive--desktop\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-1280x698.jpg\",1280,698,true],\"et-pb-image--responsive--tablet\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-980x535.jpg\",980,535,true],\"et-pb-image--responsive--phone\":[\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2026\\/05\\/crypto-feature-1-480x262.jpg\",480,262,true]},\"rttpg_author\":{\"display_name\":\"Ahmed Falah (ahmedfalah)\",\"author_link\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/author\\/ahmed\\/\"},\"rttpg_comment\":0,\"rttpg_category\":\"<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/category\\/latest-news\\/\\\" rel=\\\"category tag\\\">Latest News<\\/a>\",\"rttpg_excerpt\":\"The StablR stablecoin exploit has frozen USDR and EURR trading after an attacker minted $13.5M in unbacked tokens, violating MiCA's 1:1 backing rule.\",\"_links\":{\"self\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\",\"targetHints\":{\"allow\":[\"GET\",\"POST\",\"PUT\",\"PATCH\",\"DELETE\"]}}],\"collection\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\"}],\"about\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/types\\/post\"}],\"author\":[{\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/users\\/16\"}],\"replies\":[{\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/comments?post=37848\"}],\"version-history\":[{\"count\":1,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\\/revisions\"}],\"predecessor-version\":[{\"id\":37849,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\\/revisions\\/37849\"}],\"wp:featuredmedia\":[{\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/media\\/37847\"}],\"wp:attachment\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/media?parent=37848\"}],\"wp:term\":[{\"taxonomy\":\"category\",\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/categories?post=37848\"},{\"taxonomy\":\"post_tag\",\"embeddable\":true,\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/tags?post=37848\"}],\"wp:action-publish\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-unfiltered-html\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-sticky\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-assign-author\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-create-categories\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-assign-categories\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-create-tags\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/37848\"}],\"wp:action-assign-tags\":[{\"href\":\"https:\\/\\/coinography.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/3784